// Float varargs promotion.
//
// Before the fix, `float a = 1.5f; printf("%f", a);` printed
// 0.000000. The dlsym variadic call path in TokenCallFunc::
// compile checked `argrdp.second->is_real()` and pushed the
// Xmm with `addArgT<double>()`. For float values the Xmm only
// held the low 32 bits (movss), so printf read the upper 32
// bits as garbage zero. C ABI says floats promote to double
// in varargs — the call site must cvtss2sd into a fresh Xmm.
//
// Also fixed in the same change: TokenMember::compile's no-
// destination path used to load numeric members into a Gpq
// via safemov, which dropped real-typed members into the
// wrong register class. The real path now routes into an Xmm.
// TODO: struct-member double `printf("%f", v.x)` still
// interacts with an asmjit compiler-pass register allocator
// quirk; output depends on filename length and other prior
// float activity in the same function. Filed under the multi-
// float / struct-double printf TODO cluster. Single-local
// float varargs is the reliable case.

#include <stdio.h>

int main()
{
    float a = 1.5f;
    printf("a=%f\n", a);

    float b = 2.5f;
    printf("b=%f\n", b);

    float c = 3.5f;
    printf("c=%f\n", c);

    return 0;
}
